Python and LangChain: A Practical Overview
Python is a general-purpose programming language used to build web applications, automation tools,
data-processing systems, and artificial intelligence solutions. Its readable syntax and extensive ecosystem
make it suitable for both small scripts and large software projects.
LangChain is a framework for developing applications powered by large language models (LLMs). It
provides components that help applications connect models with prompts, retrieval systems, external tools,
and information stored in files or databases.
Python in Data Science and AI
Python is widely used in data science and machine learning because developers can combine
data-processing libraries, model libraries, and application code in one environment. Typical uses include
analysing datasets, preparing documents, calling AI models, and building interfaces around
model-generated results.
• Data processing: reading, cleaning, transforming, and organising information.
• Machine learning: preparing data and working with trained models.
• Application development: connecting services, files, databases, and user interfaces.
• Automation: performing repeatable tasks with scripts.
LangChain and Large Language Model Applications
A large language model generates responses from input text and its learned parameters. An application
usually needs additional code to prepare instructions, supply relevant context, call the model, and present
the result. LangChain provides reusable building blocks for these steps.
• Prompts organise instructions and user input before a model call.
• Document loaders read content from files and represent it as Document objects.
• Text splitters divide long documents into smaller chunks for retrieval.
• Embeddings represent text as numerical vectors that can be compared for similarity.
• Retrievers find relevant chunks, which can be supplied to a model as context.
Document Loading
Document loading is often an early step in a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) workflow. A loader
reads a source file and returns one or more Document objects. Each object commonly contains
page_content, which stores the extracted text, and metadata, which stores information about its source or
location.
Metadata can include a file path, page number, or other source details. Keeping this information allows an
application to trace retrieved passages back to their origins.
Common LangChain Document Loaders
TextLoader reads a plain-text file and returns its contents in a Document object. Specifying UTF-8
encoding helps the loader interpret common text characters consistently.
PyPDFLoader extracts text from a PDF file. In its usual page-oriented mode, it returns a Document for each
PDF page, with page-related information in the metadata.
DirectoryLoader loads files from a directory by applying a file-matching pattern. For example, a pattern
such as **/*.txt can select text files in the directory and its subdirectories. The loader can use
TextLoader to read each file.
From Files to Retrieval
A typical document-processing pipeline follows these stages:
• Load documents from text files, PDFs, or a directory.
• Inspect the extracted text and metadata to confirm that the content was read correctly.
• Split long documents into manageable chunks while preserving useful context.
• Create embeddings for the chunks and store them in a vector database.
• Retrieve relevant chunks for a user query and provide them to the language model.
The quality of the final answer depends partly on the quality of the source documents. Scanned PDFs may
require OCR, and complex layouts, tables, or unusual encodings may not extract perfectly. Always inspect
loaded content before relying on it.
Practical Notes
• Install the community loader package and PDF parser required by the application.
• Use a real, accessible PDF file path when loading PDF documents.
• Check the number of returned documents and review their metadata and text.
• For directory loading, confirm that the folder exists and that the matching pattern includes the intended
files.
• Avoid placing confidential documents in a processing pipeline unless the storage and model services are
approved for that information.